<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#braccae">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Braccae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bracae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Braccae or bracae is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word for trousers, used today for a style of woollen trousers that, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, originated among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. They were usually held up by a drawstring and reached from just above the knee to the ankles, generally longer among the peoples living further north. Roman men typically wore tunics ending at or above the knee, and in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> binding the legs and thighs with bands was taken as a sign of ill health and effeminacy. The word comes from Gaulish and is related to the English breeches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/braccae.jpg" alt="Braccae" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bullenwächter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronzefigur_Germane_Sammlung_Oberlinningen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
